WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.072
Senate accountability.

00:00:02.022 --> 00:00:04.125
The unelected chamber.

00:00:04.125 --> 00:00:12.863
One hundred and five senators, appointed by the Prime Minister, serving until age seventy-five, costing taxpayers over one hundred million dollars annually.

00:00:12.863 --> 00:00:16.318
No Canadian citizen has ever voted for a senator.

00:00:16.318 --> 00:00:22.090
Yet the Senate can block, delay, or amend any legislation passed by the elected House of Commons.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:30.159
The Senate expenses scandal revealed that senators claimed hundreds of thousands in expenses for housing, travel, and office costs — some fraudulently.

00:00:30.159 --> 00:00:44.249
The PM's ability to appoint senators means that the legislative body that reviews every piece of Canadian legislation is populated by individuals selected by one person — the same PM documented in the Carney WEF analysis as emerging from global financial networks.

00:00:44.249 --> 00:00:47.636
Senate reform has been proposed for over a century.

00:00:47.636 --> 00:00:49.840
It has never been achieved.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:01:01.056
The unelected chamber persists because constitutional amendment requires provincial consent — and because the institutional capture architecture benefits from a legislative body that operates without democratic accountability.

00:01:01.056 --> 00:01:04.215
Three structural problems with the Canadian Senate.

00:01:04.215 --> 00:01:06.863
The unelected legislative layer.

00:01:06.863 --> 00:01:10.670
The Senate is the legislative layer of institutional capture.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:19.090
The PM appoints senators from networks documented across this site — global finance, corporate boards, legal establishments, political operatives.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:25.874
Those senators serve until seventy-five, reviewing every piece of legislation that could reform the captured institutions.

00:01:25.874 --> 00:01:32.761
A senator appointed from the same network as the PM has no incentive to pass legislation that constrains that network.

00:01:32.761 --> 00:01:34.988
And no voter can remove them.

00:01:34.988 --> 00:01:44.340
Constitutional amendment to reform the Senate requires the agreement of seven provinces representing fifty percent of the population — a threshold that ensures reform never happens.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:51.272
The Senate persists because the system benefits from an unelected legislative veto that operates without democratic accountability.

00:01:51.272 --> 00:01:55.727
This is institutional capture applied to the legislative process itself.
